WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.887 --> 00:00:03.951
- Kind of happy to do some introspective,

00:00:03.951 --> 00:00:06.400
you know, really look at yourself.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.230
And boy, that guy was right.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.810
I mean, now I read that article,

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.810
I'm like, yeah, yeah, definitely.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.090
Definitely narcissist,

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:16.070
definitely trying to prove something,

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:18.100
definitely thinks he's
smarter than everybody else,

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.680
definitely arrogant, you know?

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.840
I mean, I'm going through

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.480
and I'm like, yeah,
absolutely, absolutely.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.300
I mean, he stopped just
shy of saying scumbag,

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.650
and so I'll push the
envelope and say scumbag.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.423
He could have added scumbag.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.940
- [Mark] All right, Matthew.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:39.870
Matthew, where'd you grow up?

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.240
Where are you from originally?

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.430
- [Matthew] I'm from Tampa, Florida.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:44.550
- [Mark] And tell me about your family.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.140
You had both your parents
when you were a kid?

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.610
- Yes, yes I had, yeah, my mom and my dad.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:50.810
- [Mark] Good childhood?

00:00:54.344 --> 00:00:59.344
- You know, I had a learning disability,

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:04.443
so basically I had,

00:01:05.354 --> 00:01:08.700
I had two brothers, ugh, two brothers,

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.570
I had two sisters, one brother.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.360
My mother wasn't supposed
to be able to have children.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.740
When she was 40 years old,

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.430
she went in for a hysterectomy

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.060
and found out she was pregnant

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.170
and actually then I was born.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:23.900
But I had a learning disability,
which I struggled with.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:28.160
And so as a result, my
father was an alcoholic.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.180
And I don't think I really measured up

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.670
to what he wanted in a son.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.200
I know my brother didn't.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.413
So it was just a lot of disappointment,

00:01:38.363 --> 00:01:40.623
a lot of what you would call,

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:42.971
I don't wanna say mental abuse.

00:01:42.971 --> 00:01:44.770
You know, I hate to complain about it,

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:48.863
because I've met guys
that have serious abuse,

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.250
being basically beaten and
tortured or sexually abused.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.990
So I hate to say it
wasn't a great childhood.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.350
It was a struggle.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.160
So, you know.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.573
- [Mark] And how far do you go to school?

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.430
- I eventually got a college degree.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.050
I graduated high school,

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.200
a school for kids with
learning disabilities.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.100
Basically when I graduated,
I think I probably read

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.723
at a third or fourth grade level.

00:02:14.723 --> 00:02:15.556
- [Mark] Oh, is that right?

00:02:15.556 --> 00:02:16.750
- And then I went on to college,

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.400
but I got a degree in fine arts.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.010
So there wasn't a lot of reading.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.170
And I had a registered
learning disability,

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:25.323
so I was able to take tests untimed.

00:02:25.323 --> 00:02:26.156
And you know, so I was working,

00:02:26.156 --> 00:02:30.620
I was always working around
that particular issue.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.270
- [Mark] And then eventually
you got into, into what?

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.340
- I eventually, you know, initially

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.890
after I graduated college,

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.900
I ended up getting my insurance license.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.460
And I tried to work as
an insurance adjuster,

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.970
but the truth is I couldn't
keep up with the workload.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:52.950
And eventually I started
working construction jobs,

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.340
and I ended up dating a girl

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.070
that was working for a subprime lender.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.827
And she told me, you know,
"You've got to do this.

00:03:00.827 --> 00:03:02.017
"You're charismatic.

00:03:02.017 --> 00:03:03.687
"You know, you're a natural salesman.

00:03:03.687 --> 00:03:05.410
"You would be great."

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.690
So I went and got my mortgage license,

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:11.120
and I went to work for this
company called Eagle Lending.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.120
And I actually was pretty
good as a mortgage broker.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.070
I mean, I excelled immediately

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.793
and started closing loans very quickly,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.910
the bulk of which contained
some type of fraud,

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.970
just because of the industry at the time.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:32.130
My first loan, when I went to
go send it to underwriting,

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.920
my manager actually noticed

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.050
that there was a 30 day late

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.296
on one of the documents when,

00:03:38.296 --> 00:03:40.670
my borrower had been
30 days late on a rent.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.747
And my manager told me, "White it out.

00:03:42.747 --> 00:03:45.017
"White it out, make a copy,
send it into underwriting,

00:03:45.017 --> 00:03:45.850
"you'll be fine."

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:47.530
So I did. The loan closed.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:50.200
I make 3,500 bucks real quick.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:54.140
Next guy that comes in,
he made 45,000 a year.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:57.530
If he made 55,000 a year,
he would've gotten a loan.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.030
So you change the number.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:00.630
You make a copy, you send it in.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.980
And most of the guys that were
there were doing something

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.253
and most of them were saying,
"Look, worst case scenario is,

00:04:08.127 --> 00:04:13.127
"you might get fired, but
nobody's calling the FBI.

00:04:13.807 --> 00:04:15.930
"Don't worry about it.
It's gonna be fine."

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.360
So very quickly, I think in combination

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.200
with my fine arts degree,

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.230
I got more and more creative.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:26.640
And it got to a point where,
I mean, I was closing,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.880
I was like the top producing
broker in the office.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:32.440
They then make me a manager.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.340
Then the company actually got shut down,

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.483
and I started my own company.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.880
And of course I, within
six months to a year,

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.853
I've got 12 guys working for me.

00:04:43.853 --> 00:04:44.820
- [Mark] This was around what year?

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:49.213
- I would say this is
around 1999, '98, '99.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:53.713
So I ran that company
for three or four years.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:58.520
Yeah, about '87, '88,
'89, right around '88.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.770
Sorry, 1998.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:02.789
Did I say '88?
- '98.

00:05:02.789 --> 00:05:04.900
- 1998, right.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.640
So about 1998, I think
I started the company.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.410
- [Mark] And everyone was doing
these kind of games, right?

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:12.340
- Well, yeah, they were.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.760
Usually they were massaging
this, massaging this.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.860
But I mean, I just got to
the point where I was like,

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.003
you know, there's no like,

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:24.530
fraud is black and white.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:25.690
People are like, "Oh, it's a gray area."

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:26.523
It's not a gray area.

00:05:26.523 --> 00:05:29.020
You're either lying or you're not lying.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:32.750
So if you're going to lie, then go all in.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:34.010
You know, I mean, why not?

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:36.490
I'm already in trouble.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:40.680
So I'd make a W2 or pay stub before long.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.650
As you would get caught by underwriters

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:44.620
or they would catch things,
you'd start to realize

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.800
what you needed to do to cover for that.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.190
And so I started to realize, okay,

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:51.280
if I make a fake employer,
I have to connect it

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.970
with a real tax ID number
with a real company.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.490
But then if they call the real company,

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:56.323
they'll figure it out.

00:05:56.323 --> 00:05:59.410
So I need to register in the directory

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:04.410
that this company is whatever,
a DBA of that company

00:06:04.498 --> 00:06:06.080
working in Tampa.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.350
So they'll call the Tampa number
and I can answer the phone

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:09.990
and I can verify the guy's employment.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.500
So you get more and more creative.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.970
And before you know it,
I have fake companies,

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:17.650
fake bank websites, I'm
making bank websites.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:20.130
I mean, I'm just going crazy.

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:23.863
And you know, so the
company did fairly well.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.620
The FBI said that the company,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.570
they wanted to charge me with somewhere

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.270
between like 40 to 50
million in bank fraud,

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.400
sorry, in bank fraud for
the company, this is later.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.000
But basically what happened was,

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:44.000
eventually someone I knew
got caught, and got caught.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.410
Another mortgage company
I'd been closing some

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.539
of my loans through, they got caught.

00:06:49.539 --> 00:06:52.650
The owner of the company knew me,

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:55.390
and she wore a wire on me.

00:06:55.390 --> 00:07:00.390
And I ended up being
indicted for wire fraud

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.910
and I pled guilty, but
there was no dollar loss.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.300
Like basically I'd lied on an application.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.473
So I got three years probation.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:12.750
But I couldn't run the
mortgage company anymore.

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:13.690
- [Mark] It seems like in the industry

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.640
a lot of games are being played, right?

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:17.260
- Right. Absolutely.

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:21.380
But not necessarily to the
extent that I was playing them.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:25.407
So ultimately, you know, after
I become, I get on probation,

00:07:25.407 --> 00:07:28.210
I can't run the mortgage company anymore.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:30.300
Part of my probation
was you're not allowed

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.110
to own the mortgage company.

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:32.960
You have to give up your license.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:34.750
So I give up my license.

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:37.090
And instead of doing the right thing

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:39.680
and, you know, claiming bankruptcy

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:43.240
and moving into my parents'
spare room, starting over,

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:45.740
I decide that I'm gonna take it up a notch

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:49.210
and I'm going to start creating,

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:52.320
I'm gonna go ahead and run
a real real estate scam.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.350
It was based on things I'd
learned over the years.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.743
So I start making synthetic identities.

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:03.750
And I figure out how
to get Social Security

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:05.750
to issue Social Security numbers

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:07.690
to people that don't exist.

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:09.480
I would then, you know,

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.710
I would then order secured
credit cards in their name

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:13.540
which creates a credit profile.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:14.430
I would make the payments.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:16.390
And after about six months,

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:18.310
they would have 700 credit scores.

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:20.150
So now these guys have 700 credit scores,

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:23.450
and I would attach them to employment.

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:26.910
And I would then go out and buy properties

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:30.630
in the Ybor City area of Tampa, Florida.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:31.510
I bought property.

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:35.080
I would go in and buy properties
for $40,000 and $50,000.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:36.650
But I would record the value

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:39.847
or the sale of those
properties at 200,000,

00:08:40.826 --> 00:08:44.140
195, 210, right around 200.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:47.210
And then so each one of
my synthetic identities

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:50.060
would buy like five houses.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:52.598
So it looks like they own a
million dollars in real estate.

00:08:52.598 --> 00:08:53.431
- [Mark] And these are
people who don't exist?

00:08:53.431 --> 00:08:54.264
- These people don't exist.

00:08:54.264 --> 00:08:55.570
I mean, I gave them names.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:09:00.570
I actually was a big Quintin
Tarantino fan at the time.

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:02.870
And "Pulp Fiction" had come out.

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:05.991
But I remember I loved,
I'd seen "Reservoir Dogs."

00:09:05.991 --> 00:09:10.036
And so I named the guys
were like Lee Black,

00:09:10.036 --> 00:09:13.930
Michael White, James,

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:16.250
I had James Red, I had William Blue.

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:19.290
I had a Brandon Green.

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.660
You could see the, you know,
so I thought it was cute,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.550
which by the way, the
judge saw no sense of humor

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:25.700
in that at all.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:27.670
He had no sense of humor about it.

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:31.060
So I buy these properties
and I buy them for 40,000.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:33.390
Each guy buys about five properties.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.150
I record the values around 200,000.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:36.970
So the whole area shoots up through them.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:38.460
And that way I was able
to then turn around

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:39.970
and get an appraisal on the property.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:41.480
You know, you clean up the outside.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:42.890
It's basically a crack house,

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:45.210
but you take 10 grand,
you clean it up outside,

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:46.043
it looks good.

00:09:46.043 --> 00:09:47.070
You get an appraiser to come in.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.150
He uses the comparable sales

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:50.920
which are all my comparable sales

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:54.077
and says, "You know, it's funny,
it's a shitty neighborhood

00:09:54.077 --> 00:09:57.437
"but this house over here went for 200,

00:09:57.437 --> 00:09:59.357
"this one 195, that one for 205.

00:09:59.357 --> 00:10:01.090
"I mean, I think your
house is worth 200,000."

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:03.110
Great, so he gives me an appraisal.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:04.540
I go to the bank.

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:06.953
I submit all the paperwork to the bank,

00:10:06.953 --> 00:10:10.813
and they approve the guy
for a loan for $190,000.

00:10:12.123 --> 00:10:14.023
The loan closes.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:16.570
I sign the documents.

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:17.897
I would just go and show up and I'd say,

00:10:17.897 --> 00:10:20.027
"Hey, can you give me the documents?

00:10:20.027 --> 00:10:21.860
"I'm gonna drive it out to my guy."

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:23.410
And because I knew all the title people,

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:24.510
they were like, "Sure, no problem."

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:25.410
They'd give me the documents.

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:28.770
I'd go sit in the parking lot
and I'd sign the documents.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:30.630
I'd come back 30 minutes
later, give them the documents.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.084
They cut me a check.

00:10:32.084 --> 00:10:33.913
You know, I just made a hundred thousand,

00:10:33.913 --> 00:10:36.390
$150,000 on this property.

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:38.950
And each guy did this
and I'd say on average

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:42.340
each person was worth around
half a million to 600,000.

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:43.840
I'd make the payments.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:45.750
And then eventually, I'd make the payments

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:46.940
for three or four months.

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:49.280
And then eventually my synthetic identity,

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:53.070
my false borrower would
have a tragic accident,

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:56.370
and he would stop making his payments.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:58.840
And so then when the collection agent

00:10:58.840 --> 00:10:59.780
started sending letters,

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:02.157
I would have his sister
write a letter saying,

00:11:02.157 --> 00:11:04.097
"Look, he's in a coma.

00:11:04.097 --> 00:11:04.980
"He was in an accident."

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:07.990
I'd send a copy of it
like a 12 car pile up.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:09.420
You know, he was life-flighted.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:10.253
He's in a coma.

00:11:10.253 --> 00:11:13.160
Even if he wakes up, the doctors
say he'll never work again.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:13.993
That's it.

00:11:13.993 --> 00:11:17.689
Now the collection agencies,
they have a reason why,

00:11:17.689 --> 00:11:20.160
why this guy isn't making the payments.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.570
And they don't think fraud.

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:24.170
And so they immediately foreclose

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:26.120
and they put the house back
on the market and they sell.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:28.152
They end up losing a hundred, $150,000.

00:11:28.152 --> 00:11:30.150
And they just assume, hey, we made a loan.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:30.983
The guy didn't pay.

00:11:30.983 --> 00:11:35.010
The market's, it's fickle.

00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:36.020
That's it.

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:39.200
So I think "Forbes" magazine said

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:44.190
that I had taken the market
in Ybor City in that zip code,

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:45.023
and they listed it as one

00:11:45.023 --> 00:11:48.040
of the top 20 fastest growing
markets in the nation.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:52.840
And I'd taken the medium
price from $50,000 to $250,000

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:55.137
in about 18 months.

00:11:55.137 --> 00:11:59.310
FBI said I'm responsible
for $11.5 million in fraud.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.050
And I flipped, or I did
this with 109 houses.

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:04.390
I feel like that's an
exaggeration but whatever.

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:05.890
Nobody cares about my opinion.

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:09.420
Eventually what happens,

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:12.033
and I'm also on federal
probation, by the way.

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:14.563
What ends up happening is,

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:17.959
a friend of mine who was a
sheriff's deputy finds out

00:12:17.959 --> 00:12:22.959
that, sorry, someone I know gets caught.

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:29.640
They tell the authorities what I'm doing.

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:32.300
There's, a task force is put together.

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:34.400
They hand the taskforce over to the FBI.

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:36.470
Someone that was on the task
force knew a friend of mine

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:37.390
who was a sheriff's deputy.

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:38.337
He comes to me and says, "Look,

00:12:38.337 --> 00:12:40.247
"the FBI is coming to arrest you."

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:43.973
So I take off on the run.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.300
I had about 80,000 in cash,

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:49.450
because I had to go immediately almost.

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:52.710
So I jump in my car. I go to Atlanta.

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:56.200
I rent a house from someone
with the girl I was dating.

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:57.340
We rent the house.

00:12:57.340 --> 00:13:00.110
I then go and satisfy
the loan on the house.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.525
This guy had a loan, his
name was Michael Shanahan.

00:13:02.525 --> 00:13:05.180
So I satisfy his loan.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:06.550
So I've rented his house.

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:08.510
I then assume his identity.

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:10.510
I satisfy the loan.

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:13.700
I create a fake satisfaction
of mortgage and I go downtown.

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:15.300
I satisfy the loan on the house.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:17.230
So now it looks like I, Michael Shanahan,

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:18.790
own the house free and clear.

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:20.600
I then go to three
different hard money lenders

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:23.320
and get each one of them
to lend me $150,000.

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:25.680
They lend me roughly $450,000.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:27.590
I close on all those loans simultaneously.

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:30.830
I pull out the money out of
several different bank accounts.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:32.690
And I take off again

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:35.160
before the Secret Service
enters the picture.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.530
Now the Secret Service
is interested in me.

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:39.170
They're chasing me.

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:42.390
I'm number one on the Secret
Service's Most Wanted list.

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:45.880
So I take off, the
Secret Service shows up.

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:47.053
They're looking for me.

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:52.110
And I think they named us
like the Bonnie and Clyde

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:54.630
of bank fraud or mortgage
fraud or something.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:57.200
And there's wanted posters, you know.

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.560
And by this point there's
articles coming out left

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:01.723
and right and newspaper articles.

00:14:01.723 --> 00:14:03.550
There's TV programs.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:04.650
And we're on the news.

00:14:06.034 --> 00:14:08.140
I realize it's pretty bad.

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:10.030
So I'm not thinking,

00:14:10.030 --> 00:14:11.977
I definitely think I
don't want to get caught.

00:14:11.977 --> 00:14:13.880
I, by this point, I also have, by the way,

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:15.750
I've figured out how to get the DMV

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:20.270
of pretty much any state to
issue me a driver's license.

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:22.640
So I've had, I want to say
it was 20, it's like 27

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:26.460
or 26 driver's licenses in
like seven different states.

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:29.140
I've had almost two dozen passports

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:30.880
all issued by the State Department.

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:33.330
So I'm traveling in
and out of the country.

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:34.810
We're going wherever we want.

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:36.110
We've got plenty of money.

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:40.160
End up leasing a really great town

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:42.903
or condo in Charlotte, North Carolina.

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:47.923
And I start running a
scam in South Carolina.

00:14:49.027 --> 00:14:52.610
I get someone to owner
finance their house to me,

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:53.780
a couple of people.

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:57.520
And I end up borrowing
like $1.3 million there.

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:01.010
And I'm pulling money out
of that particular scam.

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:03.100
It was about halfway through,

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:04.910
pulling out maybe five, 600,000.

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:05.743
I forget how much.

00:15:05.743 --> 00:15:07.330
I think I left 600,000 in the bank.

00:15:07.330 --> 00:15:11.010
Anyway, to wrap it up

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:12.993
is that basically one of the,

00:15:12.993 --> 00:15:17.993
an employee, it's difficult.

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:20.520
Look, they figured out what I was doing.

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.410
Bottom line is they figured
out what I was doing.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:23.947
I walked into the bank one day,

00:15:23.947 --> 00:15:26.373
two sheriff deputies show
up, they handcuff me.

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:28.860
And they sit me down in the office,

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:31.420
and the detective comes.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:34.260
And he's calling me Gary Sullivan.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:36.270
He's saying, "Well, listen,
Mr. Sullivan, Mr. Sullivan."

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:38.950
So I'm thinking they, at
first I thought they had me.

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:40.730
I'm number one on the Secret
Service's Most Wanted list.

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:41.810
They got me.

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:43.800
But they're calling me Mr. Sullivan.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:46.490
So detective shows up,
he starts questioning me.

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:48.537
He says, "Look, Wachovia's
head of security says

00:15:48.537 --> 00:15:50.647
"that you're running something
called a shotgunning scam.

00:15:50.647 --> 00:15:52.850
"They think you're committing
a fraud, blah, blah, blah."

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:55.970
And I'm like, "What, what
are you talking about?"

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:57.440
So they get him on the phone.

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:00.270
And I argue with him and the detective,

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:01.870
and I convince them.

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:04.040
I convinced the detective

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:07.730
that maybe the bank loan
officers did something wrong,

00:16:07.730 --> 00:16:09.440
but I certainly didn't.

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:12.770
So what ends up happening is
he ends up taking me downtown.

00:16:12.770 --> 00:16:14.370
He lets me fill out a police report

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:15.963
and then he lets me leave.

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:20.700
So I take off, of course.

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:22.520
Wachovia's head of security is furious.

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:24.230
Of course, the FBI Secret Service show up

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:26.730
a couple of days later, they're livid.

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:28.266
You let this guy go.

00:16:28.266 --> 00:16:32.643
I end up, basically ditch
the girl that I'm with.

00:16:33.774 --> 00:16:36.080
And then I almost get
caught by the U.S. Marshals

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:39.420
in a Starbucks, because I had gone back

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:41.160
to my old department to get a car.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:42.150
That was horrible.

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:44.570
I called the FBI, trying
to turn myself in,

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:46.700
negotiate to turn myself in.

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:47.660
That doesn't work out.

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:50.850
These guys want to give me like
seven years for just Tampa.

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:52.240
I'm sure they want another seven years

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:54.600
for what I've already done on the run.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:55.500
That doesn't work out.

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:58.613
So I go to Nashville and I
start running another scam,

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:01.640
you know, because that's what I do.

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:06.640
So I go there, I start buying houses.

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:08.630
I drive the price up.

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:11.140
I start the whole process over again.

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:16.140
And you know, what
happens is I meet a girl.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:19.210
It's always a girl.

00:17:19.210 --> 00:17:23.903
Meet a girl, she moves
in, I'm doing what I do.

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:28.610
And she and I start messing
around with another girl.

00:17:28.610 --> 00:17:30.500
And this girl comes over
a couple times a week.

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:31.333
And at some point

00:17:31.333 --> 00:17:34.660
when I found out "Dateline
NBC" was gonna do

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:36.350
a one-hour special on me.

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:38.143
I'd already been in
like "Fortune" magazine,

00:17:38.143 --> 00:17:40.190
"Bloomberg," "Business Week."

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:42.857
I'd been in probably 50 or 60 articles.

00:17:42.857 --> 00:17:45.703
"Chicago Tribune" is
doing like a series on me.

00:17:46.565 --> 00:17:48.307
But I was okay with that.

00:17:48.307 --> 00:17:52.870
What I wasn't okay with
was being on national news

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:57.040
on national TV, because any
idiot can watch "Dateline."

00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:59.130
And somebody watches
"Dateline" and sees my face,

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:03.217
and they go, "Hey, that's Carter.

00:18:03.217 --> 00:18:04.167
"He lives down the street.

00:18:04.167 --> 00:18:05.860
"He comes in and gets
a Starbucks every day."

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:08.630
Or hey, who knows.

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:12.947
So what happens is we basically
start pulling out money

00:18:12.947 --> 00:18:14.760
and I'm gonna leave.

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:16.160
I was planning on going to Australia,

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:17.500
because if you showed up in Australia

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:21.530
with a business plan and
like $100,000 at that time,

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:22.930
you weren't a citizen,

00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:24.950
but you became a permanent resident alien.

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:26.700
You could open a business
and you could live there

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:27.820
and buy property.

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:30.640
So I thought, I'll just
go there and retire.

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:31.500
So I'm obviously,

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:35.960
I borrowed another 3.5
million in Nashville.

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:37.370
So at this point on the run,

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:39.320
I've borrowed like four
and a half million,

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:40.460
something like that.

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:42.750
And so I'm about to take off.

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:47.750
And my girlfriend ends up
confiding in this other girl

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:49.570
that we're seeing who I am,

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:52.510
and she calls the Secret
Service and turns me in.

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:55.350
And bam, that's it.

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:57.350
I get arrested, and it's not good.

00:18:57.350 --> 00:18:58.580
It's a bad day.

00:18:58.580 --> 00:18:59.493
It was a bad day.

00:19:01.499 --> 00:19:03.300
Nobody's happy with me.

00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:06.010
Nobody, the judge, the U.S. attorney,

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:09.033
nobody finds what I did as being remotely,

00:19:10.936 --> 00:19:15.936
you know, sympathetic or comical.

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:19.305
I mean, like the satisfaction of mortgage,

00:19:19.305 --> 00:19:20.300
I signed one of the
satisfaction of mortgage,

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:22.795
and I did dozens of them.

00:19:22.795 --> 00:19:25.690
And one of them, I signed
C. Montgomery Burns,

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:28.010
which is the aging
tycoon in "The Simpsons,"

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:29.450
the old man that owns the power plant.

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:31.580
So, you know, these
are the kinds of things

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:34.520
that being on the run and
being kind of just creative

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:37.360
and comical and I would do

00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:40.340
that then when you're
standing in front of the judge

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:42.550
is absolutely not funny.

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:44.210
I mean, nobody's smiling.

00:19:44.210 --> 00:19:46.040
Nobody thinks, oh, that was kinda clever.

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:48.430
No, it's all bad.

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:49.430
- [Mark] Everyone thinks
you're a smart ass.

00:19:49.430 --> 00:19:50.263
- Oh, God.

00:19:50.263 --> 00:19:51.540
I mean, you know, and here's the thing,

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:52.990
and I was.

00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:55.500
And this is the thing is that yeah,

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:57.677
is that, you know, the
whole time I'm on the run,

00:19:57.677 --> 00:19:59.460
"Fortune" magazine comes
out with an article.

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:01.720
I remember reading the
"Fortune" magazine article

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:04.790
and just being offended that they'd spoken

00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:09.710
with some psychiatrist that
worked at like John Jay College

00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:12.640
for criminology in New York, right.

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:13.637
Criminal justice or something.

00:20:13.637 --> 00:20:16.677
And he was saying, "Oh, he's a con man.

00:20:16.677 --> 00:20:18.467
"And he's narcissistic.

00:20:18.467 --> 00:20:20.707
"And he thinks he's smarter
than everybody else.

00:20:20.707 --> 00:20:22.498
"He's trying to prove something.

00:20:22.498 --> 00:20:23.890
"He's trying to prove to
everybody how clever he is."

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:27.550
And I was thinking, this
guy doesn't know me.

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:29.340
And that's all bullshit.

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:30.883
That's a lie. That's not true.

00:20:31.810 --> 00:20:35.280
But this is the way, eventually,

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:38.180
like when I get to prison I
start writing guys' stories.

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:41.440
And you start kind of having
to do some introspective,

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:42.663
really look at yourself.

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:47.090
And boy, that guy was right.

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:48.300
Now I read that article.

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:50.530
I'm like, yeah, yeah, definitely.

00:20:50.530 --> 00:20:51.580
Definitely narcissist,

00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:53.560
definitely trying to prove something,

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:55.600
definitely thinks he's
smarter than everybody else,

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:57.060
definitely arrogant.

00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:58.350
You know, I'm going through

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:00.930
and I'm like, yeah, absolutely.

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:01.980
Absolutely.

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:04.970
I mean, he stopped just
shy of saying scumbag.

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:09.130
And so I'll push the
envelope and say scumbag.

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:11.180
He could have added scumbag.

00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:13.150
I'm doing scumbag things.

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:14.530
I'm doing scumbag things

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:17.976
and expecting people
to be impressed by me.

00:21:17.976 --> 00:21:18.809
You know what I'm saying?

00:21:18.809 --> 00:21:19.940
It's just like you're,

00:21:19.940 --> 00:21:24.210
it's like saying everyone
should be impressed by me.

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:28.770
I'm really just a pariah
living off of society,

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:30.010
but you should be impressed by me

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:31.510
because look at this
clever little thing I did.

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:32.563
I mean, it's just,

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:37.150
it was just pure arrogance.

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:41.070
So I end up, look, I can't
go to trial, (laughs)

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:43.350
because I'm guilty.

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:45.470
I'm extremely guilty.

00:21:45.470 --> 00:21:48.100
I remember we actually had a bond hearing,

00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:51.730
and I remember my lawyer
goes, there's a bond hearing,

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:54.340
and she said, I said,
"I'm gonna get bond?"

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:55.557
And she went, she goes, "No,

00:21:55.557 --> 00:21:56.390
"they're not gonna give you a bond,

00:21:56.390 --> 00:21:57.410
"but they have to give you the hearing."

00:21:57.410 --> 00:21:59.327
And she goes, "If they
gave you bond," she goes,

00:21:59.327 --> 00:22:00.160
"what would you do?"

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:01.310
I go, "I'd run."

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:03.710
And she goes, "Yeah, they're
not giving you a bond."

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:06.147
And keep in mind too, on the run,

00:22:06.147 --> 00:22:08.492
I'd had a nose job,

00:22:08.492 --> 00:22:10.660
two hair grafts.

00:22:10.660 --> 00:22:14.880
I had a facelift, like three liposuctions.

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:16.920
I mean, I'm going all in

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:18.780
for the whole you're
not gonna recognize me.

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:20.030
I'm not gonna get caught.

00:22:22.302 --> 00:22:25.350
Basically I end up, I plead guilty.

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:26.220
'Cause if I went to trial

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:27.440
she said they could stack the charges.

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:31.110
I could have gotten 150, 154 years.

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:32.510
I think the newspaper said something

00:22:32.510 --> 00:22:34.690
like 200-something years,
but that's not true.

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:36.320
It was 154 years.

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:37.350
Not that that would have happened.

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:39.713
They probably would've
just given me 32 years.

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:44.540
But I took a plea.

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:46.330
I got 26 years.

00:22:46.330 --> 00:22:47.863
I went to prison.

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:54.213
I mean, I will skate over,

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:58.479
you know, to explain that I got

00:22:58.479 --> 00:23:01.010
my sentence reduced twice.

00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:06.010
The quick version is that
I actually cooperated.

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:09.620
Because I can't go to trial,

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:11.890
and I can't do 26 years.

00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:14.080
And so I actually cooperated,

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:16.710
but the cooperation
didn't lead to anything.

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:18.280
No one was arrested.

00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:21.090
So it was like, you cooperated
and you don't get anything.

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:23.370
And the problem is, is you
can't force the government

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:26.320
to give you a reduction
out of cooperation.

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:28.560
It's completely at their discretion.

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:31.200
So like I said, I cooperate.

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:32.850
Nothing occurs from it.

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:35.410
I end up, one of the things

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:36.560
that the government asked me to do

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:39.030
was they asked me to be
interviewed by "Dateline."

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:40.720
Because "Dateline" had
done one, one-hour special.

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:42.730
Now they were gonna do a
second one-hour special.

00:23:42.730 --> 00:23:43.970
They wanted to interview me.

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:45.720
So I'm interviewed by "Dateline."

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:46.740
My lawyer had told me

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:48.920
it would be considered
substantial assistance,

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:51.360
and they would reduce my sentence for it.

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:52.820
So I do it.

00:23:52.820 --> 00:23:56.300
And the night before I go to be sentenced,

00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:58.097
my lawyer says, "Hey,
he did they interview.

00:23:58.097 --> 00:23:59.917
"What are you gonna reduce a sentence for?

00:23:59.917 --> 00:24:01.540
"How much are you gonna ask for off?"

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:05.030
And they say, the U.S. attorney
was named Gale McKenzie.

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:08.300
And she said, "We're not
gonna give him anything."

00:24:08.300 --> 00:24:09.647
And my lawyer goes, "You
said you'd consider it

00:24:09.647 --> 00:24:10.720
"substantial assistance."

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:13.407
She goes, "And I did
consider it, and it's not."

00:24:14.559 --> 00:24:17.370
(laughs) I just remember, oh my God.

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:20.480
And I remember thinking
that she totally conned me.

00:24:20.480 --> 00:24:23.160
She totally just bamboozled me.

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:24.540
And I remember being upset.

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:28.250
But looking back on it I
think, what did you expect?

00:24:28.250 --> 00:24:30.670
You can't bitch and moan about it.

00:24:30.670 --> 00:24:32.640
I mean, look at what you were doing.

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:35.100
You can't be a scumbag and then be upset

00:24:35.100 --> 00:24:37.730
when people do scumbag things to you.

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:39.680
So they didn't do it.

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:42.310
I get 26 years, I go to prison.

00:24:42.310 --> 00:24:43.660
They then come to me and they ask me

00:24:43.660 --> 00:24:45.900
to do a TV show called "American Greed,"

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:47.230
to be interviewed for "American Greed."

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:48.737
They said, "We will definitely consider it

00:24:48.737 --> 00:24:50.450
"substantial assistance."

00:24:50.450 --> 00:24:51.520
What choice am I gonna have?

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:53.090
They won't put it in writing.

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:54.830
They told my lawyer. I do it.

00:24:54.830 --> 00:24:55.700
They air it.

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:57.577
We go back to the U.S. attorney and say,

00:24:57.577 --> 00:24:58.527
"Look, will you reduce the sentence?

00:24:58.527 --> 00:24:59.940
"He did both these interviews now."

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:02.337
And she goes, "It's just not enough."

00:25:03.190 --> 00:25:05.050
I'm then asked to write,

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:06.750
a couple of years have
gone by at this point.

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:08.610
I'm contacted by someone

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:11.330
who teaches continuing education courses

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:12.750
for mortgage brokers.

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:14.180
'Cause they all have to
take them every year.

00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:15.930
One of them is on ethics and fraud.

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:18.640
They asked if I'll teach the course,

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:19.970
write the course for them.

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:21.519
- In prison?
- In prison.

00:25:21.519 --> 00:25:24.120
Flies up to Atlanta, talks
to the U.S. attorney.

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:27.530
The U.S attorney sends
an email to my lawyer,

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:28.830
my lawyer, they have meetings.

00:25:28.830 --> 00:25:30.810
They're absolutely gonna
reduce my sentence for it.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:32.320
Absolutely, 100%.

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:34.580
So I write the course, it's 9,500 words.

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:36.300
They're using it all over the country.

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:38.450
People are calling my
ex-wife, telling her,

00:25:39.597 --> 00:25:42.027
"I just took my continuing
education course,

00:25:42.027 --> 00:25:44.137
"and the whole thing is on your husband,

00:25:44.137 --> 00:25:45.960
"your ex-husband, and he wrote it."

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:47.882
So it's being used everywhere.

00:25:47.882 --> 00:25:49.997
So we go back to the U.S.
attorney and say, "Great.

00:25:49.997 --> 00:25:51.757
"How much are you gonna get off,

00:25:51.757 --> 00:25:52.990
"knock his sentence off?"

00:25:52.990 --> 00:25:55.700
And she says, "Nah, it's just not enough."

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:59.150
So at this point, I'm
basically, I've got 26 years

00:25:59.150 --> 00:25:59.983
and I'm doomed.

00:25:59.983 --> 00:26:01.840
When you call your lawyer and
say, "What are we gonna do?"

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:05.730
and she starts crying on the
phone, you have an issue.

00:26:05.730 --> 00:26:06.793
This is a problem.

00:26:07.630 --> 00:26:11.510
So basically it's like, look,
put in for a two-man cell,

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:14.383
join the softball team, you're staying.

00:26:15.290 --> 00:26:17.240
So what ends up happening is

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:22.092
I end up coming across this bipolar lawyer

00:26:22.092 --> 00:26:26.247
who's in prison for, he's
a rapid cycling bipolar.

00:26:26.247 --> 00:26:29.900
He's an access five rapid cycling bipolar

00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:32.250
with features of schizophrenia.

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:35.140
Since he's been a
teenager, he has believed

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:38.360
that God is telling him he is preordained

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:40.023
to be emperor of the world.

00:26:41.509 --> 00:26:43.170
What I love about it is

00:26:43.170 --> 00:26:45.640
that it doesn't affect his legal work.

00:26:45.640 --> 00:26:48.270
So he ends up becoming
a venture capitalist,

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:50.323
taking over a whole bunch of companies.

00:26:51.160 --> 00:26:52.970
And then once he's got
about 40,000 employees,

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:55.700
he starts withholding
the tax, payroll taxes.

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:58.630
He pulls out $2 million
and he starts trying to,

00:26:58.630 --> 00:27:02.139
he tries to take over the
Congo during a political coup.

00:27:02.139 --> 00:27:04.690
It's insane.

00:27:04.690 --> 00:27:06.603
So this is the guy who's telling me

00:27:06.603 --> 00:27:07.780
that God is talking to him.

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:09.390
And by the way, he
ultimately gets 22 years

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:10.223
and goes to prison.

00:27:10.223 --> 00:27:11.056
Now he's with me.

00:27:11.056 --> 00:27:12.750
But here's the great thing about him.

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:15.300
He's walking guys out the door.

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:18.000
He's getting guys' sentences cut in half.

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:19.703
He's an amazing lawyer.

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:24.890
So I go to him and I
tell him what happened.

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:26.917
And he says, "Oh, absolutely not.

00:27:26.917 --> 00:27:28.167
"I'm not gonna let that stand.

00:27:28.167 --> 00:27:29.450
"I'm gonna take care of this."

00:27:29.450 --> 00:27:31.260
He files a bunch of motions.

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:32.940
All the sane lawyers on
the street, by the way,

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:34.693
told me there's nothing you can do.

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:38.570
He filed some motions,
what's called a 2255.

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:40.170
And we go back and forth
with the government.

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:43.004
He convinces the government to reduce my,

00:27:43.004 --> 00:27:45.010
basically brings me back
in front of the court

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:46.550
and let me talk to the judge.

00:27:46.550 --> 00:27:48.537
They bring me back. They give me a lawyer.

00:27:48.537 --> 00:27:51.340
I go back. We get in front of the judge.

00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:53.583
The judge knocked seven
years off my sentence.

00:27:53.583 --> 00:27:55.160
So that's seven years.

00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:57.880
That's pretty good, but
I'm only down to 19.

00:27:57.880 --> 00:28:00.983
And so then what happens is this.

00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:05.090
I'm back, I've got about eight
or nine more years to go,

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:07.280
because I'd already been
done like seven years.

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:09.310
I got another like nine years to go

00:28:09.310 --> 00:28:10.990
with good time, whatever.

00:28:10.990 --> 00:28:15.370
So I come back and I'm
walking around the compound

00:28:15.370 --> 00:28:20.370
with a guy who was there for
stealing a hundred and no,

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:24.823
sorry, $57 million from
churches and pension funds.

00:28:25.976 --> 00:28:27.763
I think he's 60.

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:31.060
And we're walking around,

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:33.972
and he was in the middle
of actually cooperating

00:28:33.972 --> 00:28:38.200
against people that had helped
him in his Ponzi scheme.

00:28:38.200 --> 00:28:41.050
And he ends up telling
me, it's a long story,

00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:42.520
but he ends up telling me,

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:44.330
eventually just says, "Look,"

00:28:44.330 --> 00:28:46.957
he's like, "I'm afraid they're
not gonna reduce my sentence

00:28:46.957 --> 00:28:49.630
"because I've hidden Ponzi scheme funds."

00:28:49.630 --> 00:28:50.950
And I go, "But you haven't."

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:54.900
And he says, "I got a little
bit of money here and there."

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:59.443
Now why you would tell me that
knowing the position I'm in,

00:28:59.443 --> 00:29:02.290
knowing I'm ready to
cut anybody's head clean

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:04.290
off their body to get out of prison.

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:06.700
I'm just, I'm not here for the stay.

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:08.730
I want out. I don't care about you guys.

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:09.930
You don't care about me.

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:13.130
So he tells me that,

00:29:13.130 --> 00:29:14.550
and I end up talking to my lawyer,

00:29:14.550 --> 00:29:17.030
just I really wasn't even
thinking about doing anything.

00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:18.960
It was like a month later,
I'm talking to my lawyer.

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:20.502
She was sending me my transcripts.

00:29:20.502 --> 00:29:21.680
'Cause I was writing my,

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:23.810
I was in the middle of
writing my own book.

00:29:23.810 --> 00:29:25.530
And in the middle of writing
my own book, you know,

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:26.850
I needed my transcripts.

00:29:26.850 --> 00:29:28.500
So I called her and I say,
"Hey, I need my transcripts."

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:29.333
And she says, "Okay."

00:29:29.333 --> 00:29:30.166
She says, "How's everything going?"

00:29:30.166 --> 00:29:31.740
I said, "Oh, good." She
says, "Anything going on?"

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:32.573
I went like, "What do you mean?"

00:29:32.573 --> 00:29:34.177
And it was weird the way she asked it,

00:29:34.177 --> 00:29:35.130
"Anything going on in there?"

00:29:35.130 --> 00:29:37.150
I went, "No."

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:38.490
And she goes, "Anything you
want to talk to me about?"

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:40.760
I went, "No," like why would,

00:29:40.760 --> 00:29:45.760
it was very, it almost felt
like she knew something.

00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:48.587
And I went, "Well, you know," I said,

00:29:48.587 --> 00:29:49.597
"Something happened the other day,

00:29:49.597 --> 00:29:51.697
"but I don't think it means anything.

00:29:51.697 --> 00:29:53.167
"I don't think it can help me in any way."

00:29:53.167 --> 00:29:54.547
And I tell her this is what the guy said.

00:29:54.547 --> 00:29:56.510
"She goes, "Well, let me look into it."

00:29:56.510 --> 00:29:58.010
She makes a phone call.

00:29:58.010 --> 00:30:01.480
Next thing you know, they
call me into an office.

00:30:01.480 --> 00:30:03.890
The staff there calls me into an office.

00:30:03.890 --> 00:30:05.760
They asked me to talk to
a Secret Service agent.

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:07.730
I talked to a Secret Service agent.

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:10.560
So long story, I end up
explaining what happened to him.

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:11.393
They come back.

00:30:11.393 --> 00:30:13.600
They interview the people
that he left the money with.

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:15.210
They give up the money.

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:17.120
It's like half a million dollars.

00:30:17.120 --> 00:30:19.760
And they end up reindicting him.

00:30:19.760 --> 00:30:21.370
He ends up getting six more months.

00:30:21.370 --> 00:30:22.203
The two other people

00:30:22.203 --> 00:30:23.720
because they turned in
the money right away,

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:26.470
they end up getting probation,
like one year probation.

00:30:26.470 --> 00:30:30.130
Actually, one of them got,
I think, community service.

00:30:30.130 --> 00:30:30.963
Regardless.

00:30:32.748 --> 00:30:35.400
And this is the thing about that is

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:37.550
that most people won't tell you that.

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:38.801
You know what I mean?

00:30:38.801 --> 00:30:42.010
Like most people, like
90-some-odd, it's like 89%

00:30:42.010 --> 00:30:44.283
of criminals cooperate,

00:30:45.250 --> 00:30:47.450
but all of them lie about it.

00:30:47.450 --> 00:30:50.130
So, you know, it is what it is.

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:54.700
You know, scumbag, snitch, rat, whatever.

00:30:54.700 --> 00:30:56.450
I don't give a fuck.

00:30:56.450 --> 00:30:58.660
- [Mark] So did you ever feel any guilt

00:30:58.660 --> 00:31:02.430
over informing on another inmate?

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:03.263
- No.

00:31:04.188 --> 00:31:07.050
I don't feel bad about it at all.

00:31:07.050 --> 00:31:09.750
And you know, that may be,

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:12.670
I think criminals, the
bulk of criminals are,

00:31:12.670 --> 00:31:17.670
you know, sociopaths or 1%
of society is sociopathic.

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:22.330
1% of American society is
currently incarcerated.

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:24.110
So you have to assume, you know,

00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:26.570
and basically most
criminals are sociopaths.

00:31:26.570 --> 00:31:28.510
I mean, if you're gonna commit crimes

00:31:28.510 --> 00:31:30.120
and live off of society,

00:31:30.120 --> 00:31:32.830
you're probably a sociopath
if you're okay with that.

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:37.220
So my sociopathic tendencies
are self-preservation,

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:40.110
and I'm not that concerned
with the other guy.

00:31:40.110 --> 00:31:41.810
- [Mark] So the code
exists or doesn't exist?

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:44.580
- No, there is no code.

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:45.980
The code is a lie.

00:31:45.980 --> 00:31:47.230
The code is bullshit.

00:31:47.230 --> 00:31:51.030
The code is something that
other criminals tell you about

00:31:51.030 --> 00:31:54.090
specifically to get you
to not tell on them.

00:31:54.090 --> 00:31:56.340
And then they turn around and they steal.

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:58.590
When you go to prison,
they steal all your shit.

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:00.930
They fuck your fucking girlfriend.

00:32:00.930 --> 00:32:02.460
They talk shit about you.

00:32:02.460 --> 00:32:05.810
And then the moment they get
caught, they tell on you.

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:08.110
I mean, it's a delusion.

00:32:08.110 --> 00:32:12.930
The few people that
statistically, statistically,

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:17.930
based on the Federal
Sentencing Commission's report,

00:32:19.300 --> 00:32:21.110
which was about five, 10 years ago,

00:32:21.110 --> 00:32:25.020
it's like 89% of criminals cooperate.

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:26.890
And some criminals don't cooperate,

00:32:26.890 --> 00:32:28.660
because they simply don't
have the opportunity to do so.

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:30.859
- [Mark] The code is
just designed to protect

00:32:30.859 --> 00:32:33.078
whoever needs to be protected.
- Of course.

00:32:33.078 --> 00:32:37.490
- Look, El Chapo cooperating,
Maio cooperating,

00:32:37.490 --> 00:32:41.047
the heads of the cartels
cooperating with the government.

00:32:41.047 --> 00:32:43.651
How many mobsters do
you know that cooperate?

00:32:43.651 --> 00:32:47.154
Do you want to name off every
major movie that's out there

00:32:47.154 --> 00:32:50.490
that has, you know,
from "American Gangster"

00:32:50.490 --> 00:32:53.830
to "Goodfellas" to,
all of them cooperated.

00:32:53.830 --> 00:32:57.160
To survive, they're cooperating.

00:32:57.160 --> 00:32:59.080
Now, if you want to be a standup guy

00:32:59.080 --> 00:33:02.500
and you want to go do 40 years in prison

00:33:02.500 --> 00:33:05.610
and have your child
raised by someone else,

00:33:05.610 --> 00:33:09.830
and have your ex-wife or girlfriend

00:33:09.830 --> 00:33:13.210
or baby's mama be with another guy,

00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:14.690
not answer your phone,

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:16.450
and you want to be begging
for people on the street

00:33:16.450 --> 00:33:17.563
to send you money,

00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:20.710
by all means, don't say anything.

00:33:20.710 --> 00:33:22.427
But here's what bothers me is that

00:33:22.427 --> 00:33:25.880
if you're a guy who's
willing to sell drugs,

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:30.880
commit fraud, rob banks
and then get caught

00:33:31.150 --> 00:33:34.680
and tell me that you're living by a code,

00:33:34.680 --> 00:33:38.520
that you have some kind
of an ethical code,

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:40.319
the truth is, you know,

00:33:40.319 --> 00:33:42.710
that's what helps you sleep at night,

00:33:42.710 --> 00:33:44.460
but you're just a scumbag.

00:33:44.460 --> 00:33:46.697
I mean, man up and just
say, "I'm a scumbag

00:33:46.697 --> 00:33:48.257
"and I'm only in it for me.

00:33:48.257 --> 00:33:49.437
"And maybe I don't want to talk

00:33:49.437 --> 00:33:51.627
"'cause I don't want
my family to get killed

00:33:51.627 --> 00:33:53.567
"or I don't want to get killed

00:33:53.567 --> 00:33:54.917
"or I'm afraid, I got caught

00:33:54.917 --> 00:33:57.810
"and I'm going to a pen where
they'll kill me if I talk."

00:33:57.810 --> 00:33:59.690
But the truth is you're not doing it

00:33:59.690 --> 00:34:02.740
because you're a good, solid person

00:34:02.740 --> 00:34:04.800
who's out there committing crimes

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:09.110
and terrorizing decent
citizens but you've got honor.

00:34:09.110 --> 00:34:12.338
If you had honor, you'd
be working at Walmart

00:34:12.338 --> 00:34:15.210
or you'd be working in a hospital

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:18.650
or you'd be doing something
decent with your life,

00:34:18.650 --> 00:34:19.710
but you're not.

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:22.240
Just man up and say,
"I'm a fucking scumbag."

00:34:22.240 --> 00:34:24.120
I'm willing to say it.

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:26.840
And if you don't want to, that's fine,

00:34:26.840 --> 00:34:29.240
but I didn't want to
do the extra 12 years.

00:34:29.240 --> 00:34:31.940
So like I said, I'll sleep
like a baby at night.

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:34.070
And if I get called a scumbag

00:34:34.070 --> 00:34:35.310
and a snitch and a rat

00:34:35.310 --> 00:34:36.990
and I can deal with that,

00:34:36.990 --> 00:34:40.460
because I shaved 12 years off my sentence.

00:34:40.460 --> 00:34:41.433
I'm gonna survive.

00:34:42.610 --> 00:34:47.610
So what ends up happening
is they reindict the guy,

00:34:48.630 --> 00:34:49.987
and we go back to the,

00:34:52.870 --> 00:34:53.703
I end up having, they don't want

00:34:53.703 --> 00:34:55.460
to give me anything for it, by the way.

00:34:55.460 --> 00:34:57.870
They recouped half a million dollars.

00:34:57.870 --> 00:34:59.320
I actually had something
in writing this time

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:01.037
from the U.S. attorney saying,

00:35:01.037 --> 00:35:03.507
"If you recover any funds
or there's any indictments,

00:35:03.507 --> 00:35:05.210
"we will reduce your sentence."

00:35:05.210 --> 00:35:06.043
Nope.

00:35:06.043 --> 00:35:07.027
This time they go, "What?

00:35:07.027 --> 00:35:08.830
"We don't know what you're talking about."

00:35:08.830 --> 00:35:10.170
So I actually had the letter

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:11.770
they had sent the Secret Service agent.

00:35:11.770 --> 00:35:12.960
So I actually send it to the judge,

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:13.817
and the judge goes, "Oh, hell no.

00:35:13.817 --> 00:35:14.850
"What are you guys doing?"

00:35:14.850 --> 00:35:16.730
And he gives me a lawyer

00:35:16.730 --> 00:35:17.930
and I go back in front of the judge.

00:35:17.930 --> 00:35:19.200
He knocks five more years off.

00:35:19.200 --> 00:35:23.170
So I basically was about 18
months away from being released.

00:35:23.170 --> 00:35:25.873
Well, prior to that, I
had written my own memoir.

00:35:27.230 --> 00:35:28.560
So I'd written my own memoir.

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:30.710
And in the process of writing that memoir

00:35:31.870 --> 00:35:35.543
I'd started interviewing other inmates.

00:35:36.410 --> 00:35:39.550
And I'd interviewed a guy
named Efraim Diveroli,

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:43.240
which did you ever see
the movie "War Dogs"?

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:46.610
There was a movie called
"War Dogs" with Jonah Hill.

00:35:46.610 --> 00:35:49.380
So I wrote Efraim Diveroli's memoir,

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:50.900
which is called "Once a Gun Runner"

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:52.423
while I was incarcerated.

00:35:54.265 --> 00:35:56.515
And there was a movie
that was made about him

00:35:57.550 --> 00:35:58.890
by Warner Brothers.

00:35:58.890 --> 00:36:01.430
Then I ended up writing
another story about a guy.

00:36:01.430 --> 00:36:03.160
I ended up getting a book deal for that.

00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:05.513
And I get him into
"Rolling Stone" magazine.

00:36:06.360 --> 00:36:09.130
I partnered with a reporter,

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:10.770
and he gets in "Rolling Stone"

00:36:10.770 --> 00:36:12.620
and we sell the film option.

00:36:12.620 --> 00:36:14.140
We optioned the film rights for that.

00:36:14.140 --> 00:36:15.300
- [Mark] So you're writing
these stories in prison?

00:36:15.300 --> 00:36:17.209
- In prison, about other prisoners.

00:36:17.209 --> 00:36:22.193
What I realized is that these
guys' life stories have value,

00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:26.630
but they're not in a position
to write those stories.

00:36:26.630 --> 00:36:28.410
Does that make sense?

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:30.110
All right, I mean, first of
all, it's hard to write a story.

00:36:30.110 --> 00:36:32.740
It would be difficult for
you to write your own story,

00:36:32.740 --> 00:36:34.520
because the truth is
you just don't see you

00:36:34.520 --> 00:36:35.493
as you really are.

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:39.140
By reading several books
about writing memoirs,

00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:41.550
about writing true crime,
about writing nonfiction,

00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:44.950
I very quickly realize that
there are certain catalysts

00:36:44.950 --> 00:36:47.310
in people's life that affect them.

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:50.423
However, they can't see it.

00:36:51.690 --> 00:36:54.100
For instance, my father,

00:36:54.100 --> 00:36:55.940
me wanting to impress my father,

00:36:55.940 --> 00:36:57.890
who has always been disappointed in me,

00:36:59.680 --> 00:37:02.620
it's kind of, not because
of him, it's not his fault.

00:37:02.620 --> 00:37:05.580
It's my fault, but I wanted
to impress him so bad

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:09.280
I was willing to do anything
to appear successful,

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:12.100
even if it had meant commit fraud.

00:37:12.100 --> 00:37:13.410
And for a very short period of time

00:37:13.410 --> 00:37:14.660
he thought I was amazing.

00:37:17.479 --> 00:37:19.930
And I have so many stories with these guys

00:37:19.930 --> 00:37:22.760
where they weren't able
to see what had happened

00:37:22.760 --> 00:37:24.390
or how they developed over time

00:37:24.390 --> 00:37:28.063
and began committing crimes.

00:37:29.610 --> 00:37:31.990
So I, because I had kind
of gone through it myself,

00:37:31.990 --> 00:37:33.520
I see those things.

00:37:33.520 --> 00:37:36.593
And it goes back to that psychiatrist.

00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:39.010
It's like, I was upset,

00:37:39.010 --> 00:37:40.840
because I felt I didn't
see myself like that.

00:37:40.840 --> 00:37:42.930
But what eventually
you start to realize is

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:46.020
that if enough people call you an asshole,

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:48.380
you're probably an asshole.

00:37:48.380 --> 00:37:49.213
You know what I'm saying?

00:37:49.213 --> 00:37:50.850
And there's nothing wrong with that.

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:52.110
You know, does that make sense?

00:37:52.110 --> 00:37:54.020
Like, like to me, I know guys

00:37:54.020 --> 00:37:56.350
that if you said, "Bro,
you're really arrogant,"

00:37:56.350 --> 00:37:57.370
they would be furious.

00:37:57.370 --> 00:37:59.130
What do you mean I'm
arrogant? What do you mean?

00:37:59.130 --> 00:38:01.010
And they would get upset and
that's how I would have been.

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:05.260
But the truth is you're
arrogant, but I love you.

00:38:05.260 --> 00:38:06.690
You're amazing.

00:38:06.690 --> 00:38:09.600
You're a narcissist, but it's great.

00:38:09.600 --> 00:38:10.450
You're great.

00:38:10.450 --> 00:38:11.520
You know, and then that's the thing.

00:38:11.520 --> 00:38:14.500
I'm clearly, I'm narcissistic.

00:38:14.500 --> 00:38:18.543
And so, but it makes
me a great storyteller.

00:38:21.130 --> 00:38:24.140
I also feel horrible for
anybody that's a friend of mine,

00:38:24.140 --> 00:38:27.290
because the whole time they're talking,

00:38:27.290 --> 00:38:30.230
I am simply waiting for
an opportunity for them,

00:38:30.230 --> 00:38:31.800
for a lull in the conversation

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:33.940
so that I can shift the
conversation back to me.

00:38:33.940 --> 00:38:37.570
And I realize I have friends
that know this about me,

00:38:37.570 --> 00:38:41.447
and I'm so lucky because
they will stop me go,

00:38:41.447 --> 00:38:43.897
"Whoa, we're talking about me.

00:38:43.897 --> 00:38:46.357
"Give me five more minutes
to finish my story.

00:38:46.357 --> 00:38:49.450
"And we're gonna talk about
you for the next 20 minutes."

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:52.710
And you know, and it's like,
they're not mocking me.

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:54.040
They realize it's an issue.

00:38:54.040 --> 00:38:57.450
And I appreciate that
they're telling me this,

00:38:57.450 --> 00:39:01.070
because I can't do it on my own.

00:39:01.070 --> 00:39:01.940
You know how many times I've walked away

00:39:01.940 --> 00:39:06.927
from a conversation and I've
been like, "You selfish prick.

00:39:06.927 --> 00:39:09.737
"You spent 20 minutes
talking to that person.

00:39:09.737 --> 00:39:12.577
"You don't know a
fucking thing about them.

00:39:12.577 --> 00:39:15.077
"You talked about yourself the whole time.

00:39:15.077 --> 00:39:18.370
"You should be ashamed of
yourself" and it's constant.

00:39:18.370 --> 00:39:20.230
And it's horrible.

00:39:20.230 --> 00:39:23.110
It's a horrible sensation to not be able,

00:39:23.110 --> 00:39:25.660
you know what it probably
feels like, I don't know,

00:39:25.660 --> 00:39:27.255
I don't do drugs.

00:39:27.255 --> 00:39:30.010
It probably feels like what an alcoholic

00:39:30.010 --> 00:39:31.653
or a drug addict feels like.

00:39:32.520 --> 00:39:34.940
It's just this overwhelming compulsion.

00:39:34.940 --> 00:39:38.313
And as soon as it's over,
they go, "What did I just do?"

00:39:38.313 --> 00:39:40.270
They just don't even
know they're doing it.

00:39:40.270 --> 00:39:42.310
It's just autopilot.
- You want more.

00:39:42.310 --> 00:39:43.143
- Yeah.

00:39:43.143 --> 00:39:47.439
And it's horrible because
in some ways I love myself

00:39:47.439 --> 00:39:51.710
for that, and in some
ways I hate myself for it.

00:39:51.710 --> 00:39:54.250
So it's an issue.

00:39:54.250 --> 00:39:56.560
- [Nark] But the awareness
of it is a huge step.

00:39:56.560 --> 00:39:57.530
- I know.

00:39:57.530 --> 00:39:59.267
And I've had so many people say that,

00:39:59.267 --> 00:40:00.470
"Yeah, but you realize it."

00:40:00.470 --> 00:40:03.412
I know, but that almost makes it worse-

00:40:03.412 --> 00:40:06.230
- [Mark] I think you, with awareness

00:40:06.230 --> 00:40:08.693
and a little discipline,
you can police yourself

00:40:08.693 --> 00:40:11.750
and do the opposite of
what your nature is.

00:40:11.750 --> 00:40:13.690
- Listen, and I'm trying
all the time, but you know,

00:40:13.690 --> 00:40:15.711
I slip constantly.

00:40:15.711 --> 00:40:17.010
I mean, yeah.

00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:18.690
And that's what bothers me the worst.

00:40:18.690 --> 00:40:20.170
In a way, it's almost worse

00:40:20.170 --> 00:40:22.390
because now I'm so conscious of it

00:40:22.390 --> 00:40:25.130
that when I can really see it,

00:40:25.130 --> 00:40:28.170
afterwards, I'm so angry at myself for it.

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:30.089
- [Mark] I think that's what
my channel is all about.

00:40:30.089 --> 00:40:32.812
- Yeah, well, if you're
looking for flawed people,

00:40:32.812 --> 00:40:35.690
(laughs) you hit the jackpot.

00:40:35.690 --> 00:40:38.463
I just happen to be making it work for me.

00:40:39.320 --> 00:40:42.150
I was driving with my-
- You're making money.

00:40:42.150 --> 00:40:44.190
- Yeah, I'm doing okay.

00:40:44.190 --> 00:40:45.270
- [Mark] How much stress
were you going through

00:40:45.270 --> 00:40:48.370
when you were doing all
this fraudulent stuff?

00:40:48.370 --> 00:40:49.373
- You know, listen-

00:40:49.373 --> 00:40:51.410
- [Mark] Were you sleeping?

00:40:51.410 --> 00:40:53.260
- Yeah, of course, I was taking Xanax.

00:40:53.260 --> 00:40:54.093
You know what I'm saying?

00:40:54.093 --> 00:40:55.280
I mean, I'm taking Xanax to go to sleep.

00:40:55.280 --> 00:40:57.480
I'm drinking coffee all day to stay up.

00:40:57.480 --> 00:41:01.820
I'm kind of not, I'm not
really looking over my shoulder

00:41:01.820 --> 00:41:03.010
but the anxiety is there.

00:41:03.010 --> 00:41:05.030
I'm having panic attacks.

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:06.997
You know, I seem, I'm an
extremely confident person.

00:41:06.997 --> 00:41:08.210
- [Mark] So you were making millions

00:41:08.210 --> 00:41:09.291
when you were doing this?

00:41:09.291 --> 00:41:11.780
- I'm making millions,
but I'm also very unhappy.

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:12.613
You know, does that make sense?

00:41:12.613 --> 00:41:14.970
- [Mark] I mean, what kind
of numbers are you making?

00:41:14.970 --> 00:41:18.380
- Oh, I mean, look, I am responsible

00:41:20.830 --> 00:41:24.460
for 15 million specifically
for me in fraud.

00:41:24.460 --> 00:41:25.690
How much money of that was,

00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:29.060
I still owe 6 million in restitution,

00:41:29.060 --> 00:41:31.250
but I would say I'm making, you know,

00:41:31.250 --> 00:41:33.390
there would be one month
where we would rake in,

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:35.330
we'd rake in $2 million.

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:37.790
Another month we'd rake in 100,000.

00:41:37.790 --> 00:41:40.020
The next month you rake in nothing.

00:41:40.020 --> 00:41:42.150
Then you end up getting a million.

00:41:42.150 --> 00:41:44.240
There have been times, I mean,

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:46.250
I'm driving a hundred
thousand dollar vehicle,

00:41:46.250 --> 00:41:47.540
because I'm not really a flashy guy.

00:41:47.540 --> 00:41:48.920
I'm not gonna buy a Lamborghini,

00:41:48.920 --> 00:41:50.510
But I'm driving a hundred
thousand dollar vehicle.

00:41:50.510 --> 00:41:52.010
I actually have a couple of vehicles.

00:41:52.010 --> 00:41:54.110
I've got a great, huge house,

00:41:54.110 --> 00:41:56.680
a couple million dollars in the bank.

00:41:56.680 --> 00:42:00.220
Panic attacks, miserable,
everybody that's around me

00:42:00.220 --> 00:42:02.620
is only around me because
I can make them money.

00:42:03.480 --> 00:42:04.910
Nobody's a friend.

00:42:04.910 --> 00:42:06.323
I have no true friends.

00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:09.470
And I, you know,

00:42:09.470 --> 00:42:12.530
and I went to prison,

00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:15.210
and I think I had the whole time in prison

00:42:15.210 --> 00:42:19.000
two panic attacks, and they
were really just like blips,

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:20.820
like just minor, nothing.

00:42:20.820 --> 00:42:24.600
Nothing like the panic
attacks I had prior to prison.

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:27.430
And I never really felt like
I was super under stress,

00:42:27.430 --> 00:42:31.560
but I was constantly worried
and I couldn't sleep.

00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:33.570
I sleep like a baby at night now.

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:35.700
I slept like a baby in prison.

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:37.210
When I started writing the stories

00:42:37.210 --> 00:42:40.288
of my fellow inmates and publishing books

00:42:40.288 --> 00:42:45.288
and my life began to have a
purpose that wasn't illegal

00:42:45.340 --> 00:42:49.264
and I was accomplishing
something that I loved,

00:42:49.264 --> 00:42:54.264
it was like everything changed.

00:42:55.110 --> 00:42:56.960
And when people started saying to me,

00:42:59.270 --> 00:43:02.700
when I stopped saying, "Well,
I'm locked up for bank fraud"

00:43:02.700 --> 00:43:04.650
and immediately started justifying,

00:43:04.650 --> 00:43:07.307
when I started just saying, you know,

00:43:07.307 --> 00:43:08.850
"I'm a con man."

00:43:08.850 --> 00:43:09.683
So what are you here for?

00:43:09.683 --> 00:43:11.735
I'm a con man, just like that.

00:43:11.735 --> 00:43:13.153
And they would be like,

00:43:13.153 --> 00:43:16.483
"What'd you do?"

00:43:16.483 --> 00:43:18.747
And I'd go, "I stole this
much money and this and that

00:43:18.747 --> 00:43:20.377
"and bank fraud charges,

00:43:20.377 --> 00:43:23.000
"and got this much time and that's it."

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:25.660
And they would just go, listen,

00:43:25.660 --> 00:43:27.610
guys, they just don't do that,

00:43:27.610 --> 00:43:28.640
especially con men.

00:43:28.640 --> 00:43:31.840
Con men in prison are
justifying everything they did,

00:43:31.840 --> 00:43:32.800
how they shouldn't be there,

00:43:32.800 --> 00:43:35.360
and they're preparing to get out,

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:38.160
cover up everything they've
done and start over.

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:39.527
And I would talk to these guys.

00:43:39.527 --> 00:43:40.887
They'd tell me about,

00:43:40.887 --> 00:43:44.867
"Oh, there's this company
called Reputation.com,

00:43:44.867 --> 00:43:46.867
"and they can bury all the
articles and they could do this

00:43:46.867 --> 00:43:48.350
"and I could change my
name, or I could do this."

00:43:48.350 --> 00:43:49.947
And I'm sitting there
going, "You're working

00:43:49.947 --> 00:43:52.887
"on your next prison sentence right now.

00:43:52.887 --> 00:43:54.397
"You haven't got out yet.

00:43:54.397 --> 00:43:56.020
"You're coming back."

00:43:56.020 --> 00:43:57.100
Because I have a friend

00:43:57.100 --> 00:43:59.250
who's actually in there
for a double murder

00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:02.570
and super enlightened.

00:44:02.570 --> 00:44:03.720
20-something years he's done.

00:44:03.720 --> 00:44:05.720
And his whole thing was,

00:44:05.720 --> 00:44:09.250
you cannot go to prison
and continue to behave

00:44:09.250 --> 00:44:11.870
in the same manner that led you to prison.

00:44:11.870 --> 00:44:15.210
You cannot leave prison thinking that way

00:44:15.210 --> 00:44:16.610
and not expect to come back.

00:44:17.470 --> 00:44:20.500
And you know, guys, oh, I'm
not gonna get caught this time

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:21.760
or I'm not going to commit any crimes.

00:44:21.760 --> 00:44:22.593
It doesn't matter.

00:44:22.593 --> 00:44:24.630
You're still acting like a scumbag.

00:44:24.630 --> 00:44:25.920
You're still a scumbag.

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:27.580
It's going to catch up with you.

00:44:27.580 --> 00:44:28.470
- [Mark] You're a reformed con man.

00:44:28.470 --> 00:44:30.020
- I mean, yeah.

00:44:30.020 --> 00:44:32.430
This is the way I look at it.

00:44:32.430 --> 00:44:35.780
If you're an alcoholic
and you stop drinking,

00:44:35.780 --> 00:44:37.650
you're still an alcoholic.

00:44:37.650 --> 00:44:38.680
So am I a con man?

00:44:38.680 --> 00:44:41.279
Yeah, I'm constantly saying
things and doing things,

00:44:41.279 --> 00:44:42.112
you know, I'm constantly,

00:44:42.112 --> 00:44:43.330
and everybody's kinda manipulative.

00:44:43.330 --> 00:44:45.070
You know, everybody
manipulates a little bit.

00:44:45.070 --> 00:44:46.660
And I'm constantly saying things,

00:44:46.660 --> 00:44:48.890
and I catch myself a lot more.

00:44:48.890 --> 00:44:50.403
And now what I tend to do is say,

00:44:50.403 --> 00:44:52.616
"Oh, I can't promise you this."

00:44:52.616 --> 00:44:54.750
Like I try and really explain.

00:44:54.750 --> 00:44:55.870
This is what I hope will happen.

00:44:55.870 --> 00:44:57.430
I can't promise you that though.

00:44:57.430 --> 00:44:58.620
This is what I'm gonna do.

00:44:58.620 --> 00:44:59.770
Hopefully it works out.

00:45:01.090 --> 00:45:06.090
And so yeah, I end up collecting a lot

00:45:07.490 --> 00:45:08.640
of these guys' stories.

00:45:08.640 --> 00:45:09.803
I write their stories.

00:45:11.160 --> 00:45:12.863
I ended up leaving prison

00:45:12.863 --> 00:45:16.420
with a collection of
stories and several books.

00:45:16.420 --> 00:45:18.840
I started while I was in prison

00:45:18.840 --> 00:45:21.080
I actually got like a publishing deal.

00:45:21.080 --> 00:45:22.530
And that publishing deal,

00:45:22.530 --> 00:45:24.830
I really made very little
money on a publishing deal.

00:45:24.830 --> 00:45:26.480
So when I got out,

00:45:26.480 --> 00:45:29.030
I just started publishing,
self-publishing my books

00:45:30.090 --> 00:45:32.523
and writing guys' stories.

00:45:33.736 --> 00:45:36.090
I get all the stories narrated

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:37.160
and stick them on YouTube.

00:45:37.160 --> 00:45:40.210
So I got like a YouTube
channel with all them narrated.

00:45:40.210 --> 00:45:43.031
Nobody reads. It's so sad.

00:45:43.031 --> 00:45:44.950
And you know what's so
funny too about prison

00:45:44.950 --> 00:45:46.950
is that by the time I was in prison,

00:45:46.950 --> 00:45:49.630
I spent the first couple
of years just reading.

00:45:49.630 --> 00:45:50.980
And so my third grade,

00:45:50.980 --> 00:45:53.530
fourth grade reading level shot way up.

00:45:53.530 --> 00:45:55.720
I'm still a horrible reader.

00:45:55.720 --> 00:46:00.013
Look, there's so many things
that prison helped me with.

00:46:00.923 --> 00:46:05.730
And I got out and I
make my bills, you know,

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:07.790
pretty much every month, you know,

00:46:07.790 --> 00:46:10.430
make them and then some, a
little bit, not doing great,

00:46:10.430 --> 00:46:13.230
not making crazy money like I was.

00:46:13.230 --> 00:46:18.230
But I'm absolutely
thrilled, and I'm happy.

00:46:18.460 --> 00:46:21.820
And I don't have, you
know, I'm not stressed out.

00:46:21.820 --> 00:46:26.820
And I feel so much better
about myself than I ever did

00:46:27.275 --> 00:46:30.970
when I was out there
and I was making money

00:46:30.970 --> 00:46:32.380
and everybody thought I was a big shot

00:46:32.380 --> 00:46:35.777
and just absolutely not worth it.

00:46:35.777 --> 00:46:38.620
And the worst thing is is
that when you don't have money

00:46:38.620 --> 00:46:39.950
and someone tells you that,

00:46:39.950 --> 00:46:42.410
well, listen, money's
not gonna make you happy,

00:46:42.410 --> 00:46:43.800
they don't believe you.

00:46:43.800 --> 00:46:44.757
They don't believe you.

00:46:44.757 --> 00:46:46.780
But the truth is it's just
not gonna make you money,

00:46:46.780 --> 00:46:48.350
I mean, not gonna make you happy.

00:46:48.350 --> 00:46:50.827
Maybe, I guess if you're like,
if you make a bunch of money

00:46:50.827 --> 00:46:52.670
and if you made a bunch
of legitimate money

00:46:52.670 --> 00:46:54.530
in something that you loved,

00:46:54.530 --> 00:46:56.390
I could see you being very happy.

00:46:56.390 --> 00:46:58.310
Because those are the happy people.

00:46:58.310 --> 00:46:59.750
But really the happy people are the people

00:46:59.750 --> 00:47:01.640
that are just happy with what they have.

00:47:01.640 --> 00:47:04.570
Like your friend who's
married with two kids

00:47:04.570 --> 00:47:07.115
and gets to teaches
his son's little league

00:47:07.115 --> 00:47:09.520
and can't shut up about his kids

00:47:09.520 --> 00:47:13.250
and loves his job and doesn't
make a whole bunch of money,

00:47:13.250 --> 00:47:15.060
but he's like the happiest
guy you've ever met.

00:47:15.060 --> 00:47:17.690
And then I have buddies who
make millions of dollars

00:47:17.690 --> 00:47:19.050
and drive Lamborghinis

00:47:19.050 --> 00:47:22.180
and they're just miserable,
horrible, miserable,

00:47:22.180 --> 00:47:23.690
bitch and moan all the time,

00:47:23.690 --> 00:47:27.630
have everything going for them
and are absolutely miserable.

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:29.770
And I just don't want to be that guy.

00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:31.260
You know, I want to write,

00:47:31.260 --> 00:47:34.590
I want to get my stories
turned into documentaries

00:47:34.590 --> 00:47:37.910
and films and series.

00:47:37.910 --> 00:47:39.570
And that's what I'm currently, you know,

00:47:39.570 --> 00:47:40.860
that's what I currently do.

00:47:40.860 --> 00:47:42.363
I do that and I paint.

00:47:43.400 --> 00:47:45.890
So I've done a bunch of YouTube,

00:47:45.890 --> 00:47:47.260
some stuff on YouTube.

00:47:47.260 --> 00:47:48.760
And as a result of that,
I get a bunch of guys

00:47:48.760 --> 00:47:50.010
that had seen my paintings

00:47:50.010 --> 00:47:52.260
and they want me to
paint paintings for them.

00:47:53.505 --> 00:47:55.230
And that's great.

00:47:55.230 --> 00:47:56.610
- [Mark] What's the most
important lesson you've learned?

00:47:56.610 --> 00:47:57.443
That?

00:47:57.443 --> 00:47:58.276
- Oh, man, listen.

00:47:58.276 --> 00:47:59.592
You know how many times, you know

00:47:59.592 --> 00:48:01.820
how many people I know,
guys that contact me

00:48:01.820 --> 00:48:04.660
because they've seen me on
YouTube, kids, everything?

00:48:04.660 --> 00:48:05.493
What should I do?

00:48:05.493 --> 00:48:07.261
Should I do this? Should I do that?

00:48:07.261 --> 00:48:09.380
And it always boils, it's real simple now.

00:48:09.380 --> 00:48:10.760
Like everything's so simple now.

00:48:10.760 --> 00:48:14.320
It's like you find something
that you love to do

00:48:14.320 --> 00:48:16.393
and you try and be the best at it.

00:48:17.270 --> 00:48:18.610
And the money will come.

00:48:18.610 --> 00:48:19.520
I'm not saying do up dope.

00:48:19.520 --> 00:48:22.730
I'm not saying, you don't
want to live in the street.

00:48:22.730 --> 00:48:24.150
Don't be stupid.

00:48:24.150 --> 00:48:25.480
But if you have to stay

00:48:25.480 --> 00:48:27.940
in your parents' spare
room for a year or two

00:48:27.940 --> 00:48:29.780
to get it off the ground,
stay in your parents'.

00:48:29.780 --> 00:48:31.870
Stop trying to keep up with your buddies.

00:48:31.870 --> 00:48:36.570
Find something that you love
and the money will come.

00:48:36.570 --> 00:48:39.340
It's a pretty simple, you know

00:48:39.340 --> 00:48:41.560
it's a pretty simple model.

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:44.710
And if you talk to any
of these billionaires

00:48:44.710 --> 00:48:48.600
and the people that I've
met, who millions of dollars,

00:48:48.600 --> 00:48:51.030
the guys that are the
most happy are the guys

00:48:51.030 --> 00:48:53.670
that found something they
were passionate about.

00:48:53.670 --> 00:48:56.173
And then like, honestly, my girlfriend,

00:48:57.037 --> 00:49:00.280
she works 40 hours a week.

00:49:00.280 --> 00:49:02.480
And I know that she
feels like I never work.

00:49:03.480 --> 00:49:04.900
I'm really working 80 hours a week,

00:49:04.900 --> 00:49:06.427
but because I'm having
such a good time doing it

00:49:06.427 --> 00:49:08.556
and because all I do
is I paint and I write,

00:49:08.556 --> 00:49:09.530
you see what I mean?

00:49:09.530 --> 00:49:11.130
I do videos and stuff like that.

00:49:11.130 --> 00:49:14.920
So to her, I'm goofing
off, but I'm making tons,

00:49:14.920 --> 00:49:16.201
I'm not making tons of money,

00:49:16.201 --> 00:49:17.990
but I'm making really good money,

00:49:17.990 --> 00:49:21.030
pretty good money doing, I
don't even feel like I have,

00:49:21.030 --> 00:49:24.090
I don't feel like I've had
a job since I left prison.

00:49:24.090 --> 00:49:25.460
I really don't.

00:49:25.460 --> 00:49:27.980
Because I'm really just
doing what I like to do.

00:49:27.980 --> 00:49:31.120
And if it never gets better
than it is right now,

00:49:31.120 --> 00:49:33.163
it's better than it ever was.

00:49:34.970 --> 00:49:36.690
- [Mark] Excellent.

00:49:36.690 --> 00:49:38.390
Matthew, thank you so
much for talking with me.

00:49:38.390 --> 00:49:40.170
- Sure.
- Interesting story.

00:49:40.170 --> 00:49:41.130
It was a great talk. Thank you so much.

00:49:41.130 --> 00:49:43.250
- [Matthew] I hope I kept
it, that was under an hour,

00:49:43.250 --> 00:49:44.310
I hope?
- Yeah, it was great.

00:49:44.310 --> 00:49:46.070
You did a great job.

00:49:46.070 --> 00:49:47.040
You're a great storyteller too.

00:49:47.040 --> 00:49:48.848
- [Matthew] Thank you. I appreciate it.

00:49:48.848 --> 00:49:50.610
- [Mark] Yeah, that
was awesome. Thank you.

